.TH TRAIL.TOML 5 2026-10-20 "Peios trail 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
trail.toml \- trail configuration files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
trail.toml
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBtrail.toml\fR appears at every level of the tree, with a different schema
at
each. \fBUnknown keys are build errors everywhere\fR, so a typo fails loudly
instead of doing nothing.
.PP
.SS "The site root"
.PP
Required at the site root. Four keys are mandatory.
.PP
.TP
\fBsitename\fR
Type: string; Default: \fBrequired\fR; The site's identity: header wordmark
(last word accented), \fB<title>\fR suffix, search placeholder,
\fBog:site_name\fR.
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; Default: \fBrequired\fR; The front page's headline; its
\fBog:title\fR and the base for its meta description.
.TP
\fBdescription\fR
Type: string; Default: \fBrequired\fR; The front page's standfirst and meta
description; the first line of \fBllms.txt\fR.
.TP
\fBfooter\fR
Type: string; Default: \fBrequired\fR; The line at the foot of every page.
.TP
\fBurl\fR
Type: string; Default: none; The site's public base URL, for example
\fBhttps://learn.peios.org\fR. Trailing slashes are trimmed.
.TP
\fBfeatured\fR
Type: array of strings; Default: \fB[]\fR; Product slugs, in order. Sorts
products, and selects which appear on the front page. A product on a root
shelf is named by its slug alone and appears under its shelf's title.
.TP
\fBaccent\fR
Type: \fB#rrggbb\fR; Default: theme default; The site accent color.
.TP
\fBaccent_dark\fR
Type: \fB#rrggbb\fR; Default: derived; The dark-mode accent. Derived by mixing
\fBaccent\fR 75% toward white when absent.
.TP
\fBcustom_css\fR
Type: path; Default: none; A stylesheet shipped at \fB/assets/custom.css\fR,
loaded after the built-in one.
.TP
\fBfavicon\fR
Type: path; Default: none; Shipped at the output root under its own name,
linked from every page.
.TP
\fBhead_html\fR
Type: path; Default: none; A file injected verbatim at the end of every page's
\fB<head>\fR.
.TP
\fBedit_url\fR
Type: string; Default: none; "Edit this page" URL template. Must contain
\fB{path}\fR.
.TP
\fBnav\fR
Type: array of tables; Default: \fB[]\fR; Header links. See below.
.TP
\fBpassthrough\fR
Type: array of paths; Default: \fB[]\fR; Root entries copied into the output
verbatim. See below.
.TP
\fBproduct_theming\fR
Type: bool; Default: \fBtrue\fR; Whether each product tints its own pages.
.TP
\fBbuilt_by_trail\fR
Type: bool; Default: \fBtrue\fR; Whether "Built with Trail." appears under the
footer.
.TP
\fBplugins\fR
Type: tables; Default: none; Commands that expand plugin blocks. See below.
.TP
\fBmerge\fR
Type: array of tables; Default: \fB[]\fR; Other trees whose root collections join the
site. See below.
.TP
\fBlint\fR
Type: table; Default: none; The writing rules \fBtrail lint\fR checks. See
Checking style. Builds ignore it, but a malformed table fails them.
.PP
.PP
.nf
sitename = "Peios Learn"
title = "Documentation for everything Peios"
description = "Concepts, guides, and reference for the Peios operating system…"
url = "https://learn.peios.org"
accent = "#3b82f6"
featured = ["peios", "pekit", "provium", "universal-directory", "trail"]
footer = "Peios Learn — documentation for the Peios project."

[[nav]]
label = "Specs"
url = "~peios/pgss"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Validation"
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaccent\fR and \fBaccent_dark\fR must be \fB#rrggbb\fR. \fBaccent_dark\fR
without \fBaccent\fR
  is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcustom_css\fR, \fBfavicon\fR and \fBhead_html\fR are paths relative to the
site root,
  and \fBthe file must exist\fR. Naming one is also what makes it legal to
keep
  in the site root; the first path component is what is tolerated there.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBedit_url\fR must contain the literal \fB{path}\fR, which is replaced with
the
  article's source path relative to the site root (or, for a page in a
  merged tree, relative to that
  tree's root, using that tree's \fBedit_url\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Every slug in \fBfeatured\fR must name an existing root collection.
.PP
.SS "[[attachments]]"
Declare a downloadable file or a generated source archive. Each table requires
\fBid\fR, \fBtitle\fR, \fBdescription\fR, \fBsource\fR and \fBfilename\fR.
The source is relative to the declaring tree's root, outside product and
root anthology directories.
Merged trees retain their declarations; IDs must be unique across the site.
.PP
\fBformat\fR defaults to \fBfile\fR, which copies a regular file unchanged.
Use \fBtar.gz\fR to package a directory; the filename must end in .tar.gz.
Optional \fBexclude\fR entries name exact relative paths or subtrees in a
directory bundle, not glob patterns. Hidden files are included unless excluded.
File attachments do not accept exclusions.
.PP
IDs and filenames start with an ASCII letter or digit and contain only ASCII
letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens. The ID \fBindex.json\fR and
filename \fBmetadata.json\fR are reserved. Symbolic links, special files,
escaping paths and sources overlapping the output are rejected.
.PP
Use \fB~attachments/<id>\fR for the download and
\fB~attachments/<id>/metadata\fR for its JSON metadata. Valid references
are checked and work in HTML, print, Markdown and cache-busting copies.
The output is \fB/attachments/<id>/<filename>\fR; metadata is
\fB/attachments/<id>/metadata.json\fR. The directory and product slug
\fBattachments\fR cannot collide with configured downloads.
.PP
\fB/attachments/index.json\fR lists metadata records in ID order. Each
record contains schema_version (1), id, title, description, filename, url,
metadata_url, format, media_type, size_bytes and sha256. SHA-256 covers the
published bytes. Metadata omits local paths. Media type is application/gzip
for generated archives and application/octet-stream for copied files;
the hosting server controls HTTP headers.
.PP
Archives contain a top-level directory named after the ID. Entries are sorted;
ownership and timestamps are zeroed. Directories and Unix executable files
use mode 0755; other files use 0644. Empty directories are retained. Packaging
never executes source files. Content and executable modes participate in cache
invalidation; removed downloads are pruned on a successful build.
.SS "passthrough"
.PP
Root entries trail neither builds nor understands, copied into the output as
they are:
.PP
.PP
.nf
passthrough = ["CNAME", ".well-known", "ads.txt"]
.fi
.PP
Each entry is a plain relative path naming a file or a directory that exists
in the site root; directories are copied whole, nesting included. Naming an
entry is also what makes it \fBlegal\fR to keep in the site root, exactly as
\fBfavicon\fR and friends do  -  so this is how a repository that is also a
trail
site keeps its \fBCNAME\fR beside its \fBtrail.toml\fR.
.PP
Errors: an entry that is empty, absolute, or contains \fB..\fR; one naming
something that does not exist; one naming the build output directory.
.PP
Passthrough entries are copied \fBlast\fR, after everything trail generates,
so
an entry deliberately named after a generated file replaces it. Naming
\fBrobots.txt\fR is a supported way to ship your own; naming \fBassets\fR
would bury
the stylesheet, and trail will not stop you.
.PP
.SS "[[nav]]"
.PP
.TP
\fBlabel\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR The link text.
.TP
\fBurl\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR An external URL, a root-relative path, or a
\fB~\fR page reference.
.PP
References here are resolved \fBstrictly at load time\fR  -  a broken nav link
fails the build even under \fB--allow-dangling-links\fR, because site chrome
appears on every page.
.PP
.PP
every key after a table header to that table, so a \fBfooter =\fR written
below
them is read as a nav item's field and fails with
\fBunknown field 'footer', expected 'label' or 'url'\fR. Put every plain key
above the first \fB[[nav]]\fR.
.PP
A shelf is not a link target, so a nav entry cannot point at one  -  aim at
the
anthology above it, or a page inside it.
.PP
.SS "[plugins.<name>]"
.PP
One table per plugin, keyed by the name its
blocks use (\fB{name}\fR).
.PP
.TP
\fBcommand\fR
Type: array of strings; \fBrequired.\fR The program and its arguments, run
without a shell, from the site root. A program containing \fB/\fR is a path
relative to the site root; a bare name is looked up on \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
\fBanchors\fR
Type: bool; Default \fBfalse\fR. Send the plugin every page's anchors, so it
can link to targets that other pages define.
.TP
\fBafter\fR
Type: array of strings; Default none. Plugins that run before this one, whose
output's anchors join this one's anchors. Needs \fBanchors = true\fR.
.PP
.PP
.nf
[plugins.kernel-doc]
command = ["python3", "../tools/docs/trail-kernel-doc/trail-kernel-doc", "--srctree", ".."]
anchors = true

[plugins.kernel-include]
command = ["python3", "../tools/docs/trail-kernel-doc/trail-kernel-include", "--srctree", ".."]
anchors = true
after = ["kernel-doc"]
.fi
.PP
Errors:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
a name other than lowercase letters, digits, \fB-\fR and \fB_\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
an empty \fBcommand\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
a program path that does not exist;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBafter\fR without \fBanchors = true\fR, naming a plugin that is not
configured, or
  forming a cycle.
.PP
A program path inside the site root is tolerated there, the same way
\fBfavicon\fR
and friends are.
.PP
Like \fB[[nav]]\fR, plugin tables must come \fBafter every plain key\fR, since
TOML
gives any key below a table header to that table. In what order \fB[[nav]]\fR,
\fB[plugins.*]\fR and \fB[[merge]]\fR come relative to each other does not
matter.
.PP
In a merged tree's \fBtrail.toml\fR,
"the site root" in all of the above means that tree's root: its plugins
resolve and run there.
.PP
.SS "[[merge]]"
.PP
One table per merged tree, in the
order its plugins and passthrough entries yield to one another.
.PP
.TP
\fBpath\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR The tree's directory, relative to the site root.
.PP
.PP
.nf
[[merge]]
path = "../kernel/docs"
.fi
.PP
Every \fB*.product\fR and \fB*.antho\fR at the tree root or on a root shelf joins the site. A merged tree's
\fBtrail.toml\fR is
optional; when present it must be valid, and only its \fBedit_url\fR,
\fBpassthrough\fR, \fBattachments\fR and \fBplugins\fR apply. Everything else, including its own
\fB[[merge]]\fR list, is ignored.
.PP
Errors:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
an empty \fBpath\fR, or one that is not a directory;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBpath\fR that is the site root itself;
.IP \(bu 2
conflicting container types, metadata, or sibling orders;
.IP \(bu 2
duplicate article slugs, alias slugs, or image destinations;
.IP \(bu 2
anything in the tree's root that would be an error in a site root, except a
  \fBdist/\fR directory.
.PP
A \fBpath\fR inside the site root is tolerated there, the same way
\fBfavicon\fR and
friends are.
.PP
Matching containers combine recursively at every supported level: products,
anthologies, shelves, topics, topic folders, books, and nested chapters.
Containers match by slug within the same parent, including shelf boundaries.
Their types, orders, and resolved metadata must agree, including titles,
descriptions, reference claims, product branding, and book short names.
Omitted optional metadata uses its usual default before comparison.
Leaf articles and aliases never replace existing entries, even if identical.
Different siblings must have distinct orders and slugs. Shelves still share
their parent's URL space. Root shelves with the same slug combine and must
agree on order and title; a collection must sit on the same root shelf, or
on none, in every tree that contributes to it.
.PP
Article and image source paths stay in their original trees. Edit links,
relative image references, and plugin requests use those source locations.
Merge order does not change authored navigation order.
.PP
.SS "What url unlocks"
.PP
Without it: no \fBsitemap.xml\fR, no \fBSitemap:\fR line in \fBrobots.txt\fR,
no \fBog:url\fR,
relative canonical URLs, and relative outward links in \fB/print\fR bundles.
Set
it before deploying.
.PP
.SS "A product"
.PP
Required in every \fB<slug>.product\fR directory.
.PP
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR The product's display name.
.TP
\fBmonogram\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR The short string on the product's tile.
.TP
\fBicon\fR
Type: string; An SVG file beside this \fBtrail.toml\fR, shown in place of
the monogram tile. Its root \fB<svg>\fR needs the SVG namespace and no
\fBid\fR. It is published at \fB/assets/icons/<slug>.svg\fR. Merged trees
need not repeat it; those that set it must have identical SVGs.
.TP
\fBcolor\fR
Type: \fB#rrggbb\fR; \fBrequired.\fR The product accent.
.TP
\fBdescription\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR One sentence: the card text, the page
standfirst, the \fBllms.txt\fR entry.
.TP
\fBinline_ref\fR
Type: array of strings; Phrases linking to the product's landing page.
.PP
.PP
.nf
title = "pekit"
monogram = "pk"
color = "#22c55e"
description = "The recipe-driven build and packaging tool."
inline_ref = ["pekit"]
.fi
.PP
.SS "An anthology"
.PP
Required in every \fB.antho\fR directory, including a root
\fB<slug>.antho\fR collection. Root anthologies need no order prefix or
product branding fields. They publish at \fB/<slug>\fR and participate in
\fBfeatured\fR alongside products. Navigation uses a monogram derived from
the title and the default Trail accent. The \fBproducts\fR array in
\fBsite.json\fR includes both root types; its \fBkind\fR field is
\fBproduct\fR or \fBanthology\fR.
.PP
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR
.TP
\fBdescription\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR The card text, the page standfirst, the
\fBllms.txt\fR entry.
.TP
\fBinline_ref\fR
Type: array of strings; Phrases linking to the anthology's landing page.
.PP
.SS "A book"
.PP
Required in every \fB.book\fR directory.
.PP
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR
.TP
\fBdescription\fR
Type: string; \fBrequired.\fR
.TP
\fBshort\fR
Type: string; An abbreviation used where the full title will not fit: the
sidebar heading, the card pill, the line above the cover title.
.TP
\fBinline_ref\fR
Type: array of strings; Phrases linking to the book's cover. These can carry a
\fBsection \fR section suffix in prose.
.PP
.PP
.nf
title = "Peios Generic System Standards"
short = "PGSS"
description = "Cross-platform system standards from the Peios Project"
inline_ref = ["PGSS", "Peios Generic System Standards"]
.fi
.PP
.SS "A topic, subfolder, or chapter"
.PP
Optional. Both keys are optional.
.PP
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; Overrides the slug-derived title.
.TP
\fBinline_ref\fR
Type: array of strings; Phrases linking to the container's \fBfirst
article\fR. Declaring them on a container with no articles is an error.
.PP
.PP
.nf
title = "Reference"
.fi
.PP
.SS "A shelf"
.PP
Optional, and takes \fBtitle\fR only.
.PP
.TP
\fBtitle\fR
Type: string; Overrides the slug-derived title.
.PP
\fB\fBinline_ref\fR on a shelf is an error.\fR A shelf has no page and no
articles
of its own, so the phrase would have nowhere to land  -  declare it on the
shelf's items instead. The same applies to a root shelf.
.PP
.SS "Derived titles"
.PP
Wherever \fBtitle\fR is optional and absent, it comes from the slug: hyphens
become spaces, and the words take title case. The first and last words and
every major word are capitalised. Between them, these stay lowercase:
.PP
.IP \(bu 2
articles: a, an, the;
.IP \(bu 2
coordinating conjunctions: and, but, or, nor, for, so, yet;
.IP \(bu 2
"as";
.IP \(bu 2
prepositions, such as to, of, in, on, at, by, from, with, about, between,
  through and without.
.PP
So \fB2--writing-tests.topic\fR becomes "Writing Tests",
\fBdeveloping-for-peios\fR
becomes "Developing for Peios", and \fBwhat-an-event-is\fR becomes "What an
Event
Is".
.PP
Words that are as often adverbs or particles as prepositions, such as "up",
"off" and "over", are capitalised: a slug can't say which it means, and
"Setting Up the Network" needs the capital.
.PP
Write the key when the derived title is wrong. A slug is lowercase, so it
can't carry acronyms, proper nouns or a name's own casing ("GXWI", "netd").
.PP
.SS "inline_ref everywhere"
.PP
The key means the same thing at every level: a list of phrases that should
become links to this thing wherever they appear in the site's prose. Phrases
are exclusive site-wide  -  two declarers claiming one phrase is an error  -
and
must not be empty, carry leading or trailing whitespace, or contain \fBsection
\fR. See
Inline references.
.SS "Writing rules"
.PP
The optional \fB[lint]\fR table configures the separate lint command.
See trail-lint(5) for rule names, word lists, defaults, and suppressions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trail (1),
.BR trail.toml (5)
.PP
Trail Learn: https://learn.peios.org/trail/
